WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:08.280
What does it mean… for a world to have died?

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.880
Does it have to be desolate, does it have
to lie in ruins, or could it be beautiful,

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.430
still rich with green, still haunted by the
rare remaining lifeform?

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:24.990
I ask these questions, because the game Gris…
is quite possibly the most visually breathtaking

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.020
thing I’ve ever played.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:32.649
The world is incredible — the colors, the
creatures, the landscapes!

00:00:32.649 --> 00:00:35.730
It’s all so… alive.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:49.030
Which is important, because I believe the
world of Gris… is a world that has died.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:53.469
When we think of ‘dead worlds,’ we typically
don’t think of beauty.

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:58.030
This is especially true for video games — where
the word ‘dead’ usually implies a setting

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.670
is lacking in meaningful content.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:05.049
But I want to challenge this assumption, to
explore environments that are spectacular

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:10.750
and filled with artistic merit, yet are, in
their own ways, dead.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:16.860
For sometimes the end of the world is just
the beginning…

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.810
Speaking of which, Gris begins with the end
of the world.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:26.210
The protagonist, living in a landscape of
color upon a mysterious stone hand, finds

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.790
this realm collapsing around her.

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:33.680
Falling through the sky, she descends all
the way to the surface, where nothing remains

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.020
but a monochrome, desolate expanse.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:42.680
From here, the world is split into five sections
that reflect the five stages of grief.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.420
Each region is further centered on a dominant
color, with gray representing the initial

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.560
detachment of denial.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.860
Recovering from the fall, you wander through
a graveyard of bleak ruins — perhaps wreckage

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.960
from the recent calamity, or perhaps remnants
from an age lost to time.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.890
The environments of Gris are not meant to
be completely literal, the external desolation

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:11.790
often a symbolic representation of the main
character’s inner sorrow.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:16.980
The opening of Gris can be better understood
in conversation with this… other game about

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.110
personal loss personified on a vast, impersonal
scale.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:26.250
Shadow of the Colossus is the kind of title
that looms over the entire medium.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:31.670
The iconic story of a lone warrior defeating
sixteen leviathans to bring a loved one back

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:36.500
to life — the game does not, at a glance,
take place in a ‘dead world’.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:37.500
I mean…

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:43.300
there are giant creatures wandering around
in it, hard to get more ‘alive’ than that,

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.300
right?

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:49.690
Yet the Forbidden Lands — the forgotten,
cursed region where the game takes place —somehow

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.380
manage to feel… blank.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.250
The kind of blankness that hollows you out.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:03:01.019
It takes so long to travel to each colossus
that the towering giants start to seem hopelessly

00:03:01.019 --> 00:03:04.720
minuscule next to the bleak enormity of the
land itself.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.440
There’s an argument to be made that this
feeling of blankness was even more profound

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.800
in earlier versions of the game due to the
limited graphical detail, but even in the

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:19.510
remastered version, the ‘wrongness’ this
land is unmistakable, like this is a world

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:21.060
that life has forsaken.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:26.890
Though often beautiful, it is, in its own
way, a dead world.

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:31.680
But Shadow of the Colossus has more in common
with the first stage of Gris than just its

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:32.680
setting.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:36.580
It is also, quite overtly, a story about denial.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:40.799
It is clear from the opening sequence that
this deal the main character has struck to

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:44.069
resurrect their loved one is… extremely
dodgy.

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:49.870
And yet it’s easy for both the protagonist
and the player to ignore any feelings of unease,

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:52.520
because there are monsters to fight!

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:57.550
But as the game wears on, you start to think
more and more about how most of these creatures

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.730
only become aggressive if you attack first.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:04.900
I’m far from the first person to make this
point, but because of the environment’s

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.600
bareness, you’re left alone with your thoughts
between each battle.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:12.569
This makes it harder to hide from the fact
that you’re extinguishing some of the last

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:18.010
remaining beings in this world — all because
you can’t let go of a personal loss.

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:23.240
…If you return to a spot where you slew
a colossus, you will find their bodies have

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:28.770
become part of the landscape, the surroundings
sweeping in to reclaim these last vestiges

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:29.860
of life.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:35.220
It is impossible, when exploring these remains,
to hide from the reality of what you’ve

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:36.220
done.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:40.920
Though you, like the protagonist, want to
keep denying that the world — their world

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:48.220
— is really dead, the physicality of these
bodies makes the truth of bereavement undeniable.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:52.650
It's not clear, at first, whom the protagonist
of Gris is grieving for.

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:58.240
But it is clear she is grieving for someone
who was central to her emotional world, their

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:04.419
significance quite literally embodied in the
colossal statues littered throughout the land.

00:05:04.419 --> 00:05:09.710
That disintegrating hand in the opening represented
their passing, yet they stood so tall in the

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:14.340
protagonist’s life that their remains continue
to shape the environment long after their

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.010
death.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:22.180
The way death alters the landscapes of these
games reminds me of a real natural phenomenon.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:27.669
When a whale swims its last, the carcass will
sometimes sink all the way to the ocean floor

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:30.310
and create something called a ‘whalefall.’

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:35.600
The impact of a whalefall on the abyssal ecosystem
is, well, colossal.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:41.550
A single carcass can bring more organic material
to the seafloor than two hundred years of

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.789
nutrients falling from the surface.

00:05:43.789 --> 00:05:48.690
Like grief, whalefalls too can be divided
into specific stages.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:54.710
In the initial Scavenger Stage, specialized
foragers pillage the leviathan from all directions.

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:59.300
After playing Shadow of the Colossus, I think
I kind of know what that feels like.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:04.640
For the next two years, the whale’s remains
will become animated with movement — a tumultuous

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.970
new ecosystem formed from the remains of a
dead one.

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:14.090
The world of Gris, likewise, doesn’t remain
gray forever.

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:20.220
And as denial turns to anger, harsh splotches
of red seep into the surrounding environment.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:26.919
You slide, emboldened with reckless fury,
down a steep slope into an unforgiving desert.

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:32.430
Ruins streak these maroon sands, the jutting
spires like the tips of spears left over from

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:34.470
some ancient battle.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:39.770
Storms also sweep through this region, watercolor
swirls of blood-red that make it wise to seek

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:40.770
shelter.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:45.800
Yet critically, the game gives you the option
to face these storms head-on, and though you’ll

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:51.300
inevitably be blown backwards, it’s difficult
to resist the temptation to charge blindly

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:56.389
forwards, and become angry at the world itself…

00:06:56.389 --> 00:07:00.979
The game Hollow Knight similarly encourages
you to think combatively.

00:07:00.979 --> 00:07:05.870
With its punishing difficulty and aggressive
enemies, the game makes it feels like it’s

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:07.539
you against the world.

00:07:07.539 --> 00:07:12.819
Set in the fallen kingdom of Hallownest, Hollow
Knight’s ecosystem is plagued by a supernatural

00:07:12.819 --> 00:07:19.030
illness known simply as the Infection — an
apocalyptic epidemic that manifests in the

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:23.030
form of rageful insects and great pustules
of disease.

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:28.080
The background design can make the entire
world feel like a kind of infection, an unwanted

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:31.370
growth upon a long-dead exoskeleton.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:37.599
Yet Hollow Knight is also full of quiet expressions
of endurance, perseverance even in the grimmest

00:07:37.599 --> 00:07:38.599
environments.

00:07:38.599 --> 00:07:42.509
Though nominally a game about violence, I
think it’s also a game about rebirth, and

00:07:42.509 --> 00:07:45.270
how beauty can emerge in the toughest conditions.

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:52.039
In the second stage of Gris, you can encounter,
in the shelters, creatures living inside chunks

00:07:52.039 --> 00:07:53.110
of ruins.

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:57.490
Like the denizens of Hollow Knight, one could
interpret these lifeforms — which come in

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:04.770
all shapes and sizes — as infections, unwanted
parasites plaguing the corpse of a lost civilization.

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:09.380
But there’s also something poignant here,
a testament to adaptability in the face of

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:14.580
adversity, one that can help you appreciate
the value of knowing when to take shelter.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:19.169
Perhaps a version of the world has perished,
but if you let go of your anger, you can see

00:08:19.169 --> 00:08:22.419
that new life has taken root.

00:08:22.419 --> 00:08:28.129
With the protagonist’s anger subsiding,
the world transforms in an eruption of green.

00:08:28.129 --> 00:08:33.640
Enormous vines snake across the landscape,
leading down far below another layer of clouds

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:36.460
to a vast and inviting forest.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:41.090
Your grief has now entered the bargaining
stage, and it’s easy to feel like your sorrows

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:46.570
will no longer shape the world, that there
will be no traces of decay in this landscape.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:52.029
Yet even in the midst of the forest, the shells
of abandoned buildings still remain, the remnants

00:08:52.029 --> 00:08:55.089
overrun with encroaching plant life.

00:08:55.089 --> 00:09:00.610
…How should we feel when we see cities reclaimed
by nature?

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:06.000
It’s a question that was on my mind a lot
when I played Cloud Gardens, a peaceful gardening

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.990
simulator crossed with a… dystopian landscape
builder, I guess?

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:16.930
In this game, you help plants swallow up the
wreckage of humanity, an activity that elicits…

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:18.519
complicated emotions.

00:09:18.519 --> 00:09:23.370
On the one hand, Cloud Gardens has a charming
voxel aesthetic that makes turning decrepit

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:27.370
cityscapes into bastions of life quite satisfying.

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:29.150
But on the other hand, well…

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:34.610
there’s definitely no sign of living people
in these landscapes.

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:40.029
In his book “The World Without Us,” American
journalist Alan Weisman explores what would

00:09:40.029 --> 00:09:45.149
theoretically happen to human structures and
objects if we all suddenly disappeared.

00:09:45.149 --> 00:09:51.240
And the answer, for the most part, is that
nature would erase all traces of humanity

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.310
extremely quickly.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:56.720
Most buildings would be gone in just a few
hundred years.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:00.990
Certain plastics and metals would linger a
little longer… but not forever.

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:06.320
On a geological timescale, our built environments
would be gone in the blink of an eye.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:11.459
There is a kind of mental bargaining that
goes on when I try to decide how such facts

00:10:11.459 --> 00:10:12.860
make me feel.

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:17.570
Is it comforting to know that new life would
grow from the skeleton of humanity if our

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:19.519
species were to go extinct?

00:10:19.519 --> 00:10:24.420
Or is it terrifying to imagine being forgotten
so easily?

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:27.940
There are no other humans left in the forest
of Gris.

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:33.590
But there is a little robot, who you, fittingly,
have to literally bargain with to find a way

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:34.600
out of the woods.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:37.880
It’s a sweet change of pace from your lonesome
travels.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:42.089
Yet something about the way the robot knows
all the ins and outs of the forest can make

00:10:42.089 --> 00:10:46.649
you feel like it belongs in this reborn world
in a way that you just…

00:10:46.649 --> 00:10:47.649
don’t.

00:10:47.649 --> 00:10:53.079
The world you knew is gone, and no amount
of bargaining will change that.

00:10:53.079 --> 00:10:57.821
As the protagonist floats once more in the
palm of the person she lost, the landscape

00:10:57.821 --> 00:11:02.780
changes a fourth time and the long melancholy
of sadness begins.

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:07.459
This stage of grief is represented, unsurprisingly,
by shades of blue.

00:11:07.459 --> 00:11:13.279
Rain begins to fall like teardrops as you
strike out through a flooded watercolor landscape.

00:11:13.279 --> 00:11:18.361
Yet while the surface of this region is gloomy,
if you dive under the waves, you’ll find

00:11:18.361 --> 00:11:20.370
a thriving ecosystem of fish.

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:22.899
It’s a jarring transition.

00:11:22.899 --> 00:11:28.459
How can this ‘dead world,’ a world in
the wake of a calamity, be so full of life?

00:11:28.459 --> 00:11:33.959
I don’t think I’ve ever seen a setting
more vibrant than the one in Abzu.

00:11:33.959 --> 00:11:39.630
A game where you steer a diver through gorgeous
undersea environments, the ecosystem is just

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:41.290
overflowing with movement.

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:47.310
I genuinely don’t even know how it’s possible
for a title from 2016 to simulate this many

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:49.529
schools of fish at a time.

00:11:49.529 --> 00:11:54.529
If you just stop the camera, anywhere, the
sheer amount of marine life that will swim

00:11:54.529 --> 00:11:56.130
by is just insane.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:12:01.180
It’s all so kinetic, so interactive, so
full of vitality.

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:06.450
I don’t know that there’s ever been a
world more emphatically alive than this one.

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:10.570
And yet the world of Abzu is haunted by death.

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:15.560
From early in the game, you can stumble upon
murals from a doomed civilization.

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:21.220
Each fragment tells of a culture that fell,
of a people whose memory is lost to the waves,

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:24.769
their crumbling halls now home to creatures
of the deep.

00:12:24.769 --> 00:12:30.940
There are ruins in the ocean of Gris as well,
sunken temples overgrown with forests of coral.

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:36.870
It reminds me of the second stage of a whalefall,
when groves of lifeforms known as ‘zombie

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:38.990
worms’ spread their roots into the bones.

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:45.920
They pick the skeleton completely clean, making
it hard to imagine the animal as it once was.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:50.470
But there’s a moment in Abzu that conveys
the splendor of a living whale better than

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:53.030
perhaps any other piece of media.

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:58.460
It is, at first, a tranquil moment where you
swim with a mother and calf through the open

00:12:58.460 --> 00:12:59.460
ocean.

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:05.180
And as you swim, an entire pod of whales joins
your dive, opening their mouths wide to take

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:07.560
huge gulps of krill.

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:13.361
And then they swim on, and you think the moment
is over — until another pod of whales, even

00:13:13.361 --> 00:13:16.639
larger than the first, comes in from the other
direction.

00:13:16.639 --> 00:13:21.980
Before you even have a chance to fully process
the sight — this happens.

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:28.950
A blue whale emerges from offscreen, its eye
right next to you, just staring.

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:33.110
And then the camera pulls all the way back,
and you realize you’re in the midst of another

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:38.670
pod, and they all start spinning through the
depths, brimming with life and majesty.

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:44.200
And then, at last, this pod departs as well,
and you realize you’ve accidentally traveled

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:47.240
all the way down to the very bottom of the
ocean.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:52.649
And what you find down here is a vast boneyard
of whales forgotten on the sea floor.

00:13:52.649 --> 00:13:56.779
It’s a real tonal shift, that can be read
as pessimistic.

00:13:56.779 --> 00:14:01.570
But I think that would be doing this moment
a disservice, because even though these skeletons

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:04.709
are picked clean — that doesn’t mean they’re
lifeless.

00:14:04.709 --> 00:14:09.910
In the third and final stage of a whalefall,
the bones appear deserted, but they’re actually

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:14.870
full of bacteria that produce vital sulfides
to the surrounding ecosystem.

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:19.649
Other forms of life can flourish around a
whalefall for up to half a century — sometimes

00:14:19.649 --> 00:14:22.980
longer than the lifespan of whales themselves.

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:28.269
Maybe the bionetwork of Abzu is built upon
remains, but it is likely because of these

00:14:28.269 --> 00:14:32.449
remains that so much life has gathered in
one place.

00:14:32.449 --> 00:14:38.000
Even in the best of circumstances, however,
the presence of any skeleton implies a cause

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:39.000
of death.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:45.040
For worlds, this typically means some kind
of apocalyptic event, the sort of big destructive

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:49.190
moment that disaster movies love to show us
in gratuitous detail.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:54.950
I think it’s important, therefore, that
you never see the calamity that sank the civilization

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:56.050
in Abzu.

00:14:56.050 --> 00:15:00.630
You also never learn what emptied the world
in Cloud Gardens, or made the cursed lands

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:04.019
in Shadow of the Colossus, you know, cursed.

00:15:04.019 --> 00:15:09.440
Even Gris, which is about the passing of a
specific person as much as the world, never

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:12.860
gives us any insight into the demise itself.

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:16.500
In the context of these narratives, it doesn’t
really matter.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:19.970
What matters is what happens next.

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:25.500
If any force in Gris could be labeled apocalyptic,
it would be the tendrils of shadow that haunt

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:27.950
the protagonist throughout her journey.

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:32.870
Likely a representation of grief’s most
destructive tendencies, this darkness constantly

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:35.750
threatens to overwhelm the main character.

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:40.990
Like sorrow itself, it knows no boundaries,
following you into any environment.

00:15:40.990 --> 00:15:46.300
In one underwater section, it becomes a giant
eel that pursues you through tight corridors

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:49.430
in one of the tensest chase scenes I’ve
witnessed.

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:55.610
Like death, it seems inescapable, able to
split itself into two to cut off your escape,

00:15:55.610 --> 00:16:01.240
or transform into a thousand little eels that
chase you into the blackness…

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:07.340
One of the grimmest dead worlds I’ve seen
is ‘There Will Come Soft Rains,’ a 1984

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:09.269
Soviet animated short film.

00:16:09.269 --> 00:16:14.420
Based on a Ray Bradbury story of the same
name, the narrative follows the automatic

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:19.970
systems of a house continuing their pre-programed
daily routines, despite the fact the people

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:22.820
of the house are… ashes.

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:27.459
It is revealed that this building is the last
one standing on a planet that has otherwise

00:16:27.459 --> 00:16:30.390
been obliterated by nuclear war.

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:35.461
The title for ‘There Will Come Soft Rains’
comes from a poem by Sara Teasdale, which

00:16:35.461 --> 00:16:41.569
the house recites part of at the end of the
story: “Not one would mind, neither bird

00:16:41.569 --> 00:16:49.720
nor tree, / If mankind perished utterly; / And
Spring herself, when she woke at dawn, / Would

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:52.970
scarcely know that we were gone.”

00:16:52.970 --> 00:16:54.600
Though not exactly…

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:59.420
uplifting, this poem, like Bradbury’s short
story, is more tranquil than the animated

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:00.420
short.

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:05.020
I think there is a kind of comfort one can
take in this idea of nature continuing on

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:10.250
without us, particularly in 1918 when the
poem was written, against the backdrop of

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:11.730
the First World War.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:16.939
The poem, and many of the games in this video,
imply that if a version of the world comes

00:17:16.939 --> 00:17:21.350
to an end, another will take its place.

00:17:21.350 --> 00:17:23.780
Even in the darkness, there is light.

00:17:23.780 --> 00:17:29.250
The very last color of Gris is an illuminating
shade of yellow, representing the stage of

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:30.250
acceptance.

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:37.200
Significantly, however, this final stage does
not return the world to a pre-ruined state.

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:40.980
The architecture, though brighter, is still
withered and overgrown with plants.

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:45.840
Acceptance doesn’t mean returning to the
way things were, just recognizing them as

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:46.840
they are.

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:52.429
I don’t think any game exemplifies this
concept better than Sable, the final title

00:17:52.429 --> 00:17:54.410
I want to discuss today.

00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:59.789
Set within a harsh desert, Sable has, on the
surface, the most conventionally desolate

00:17:59.789 --> 00:18:02.960
post-apocalyptic world in this entire video.

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:07.990
But it is not, to a remarkable degree, a game
interested in mourning the past.

00:18:07.990 --> 00:18:13.470
None of the people you speak to are wistful
of ages gone, or lamenting the desolation

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:14.590
that surrounds them.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:19.300
Though there isn’t as much wildlife in this
land as most of the games in this video, people

00:18:19.300 --> 00:18:22.890
delight in the animals they have all the more.

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:27.730
Everyone’s satisfaction, presumably, is
because so much time has passed, no one remembers

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:29.410
the way things used to be.

00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:33.240
The world has mourned, and now, it has moved
on.

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:38.070
Indeed, as a coming-of-age story, Sable’s
narrative is deeply focused on the future,

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:41.790
on what’s waiting over the next horizon.

00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:46.960
I think it’s fitting that the largest structure
in Sable is a set of ruins nicknamed ‘The

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:47.960
Whale.’

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:52.280
Implied to have once been part of an interstellar
ship, these ruins have been pulled apart for

00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:54.669
building materials to make new houses.

00:18:54.669 --> 00:18:59.640
Likewise, there are theories that there is
actually a secret fourth stage to whalefalls

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:03.660
where their skeleton provides a structure
for new coral reefs.

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:08.159
Since scientists have only been researching
whalefalls for a few decades, it’s currently

00:19:08.159 --> 00:19:13.080
unknown how far into the future this stage
could sustain life.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:14.800
Is a whalefall really dead?

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:20.101
I mean, certainly the whale is dead — that
original lifeform will never swim again, no

00:19:20.101 --> 00:19:24.669
matter how positive we want to be about its
role in the circle of life.

00:19:24.669 --> 00:19:30.320
I don’t want to trivialize the idea of a
dead world.

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:35.010
Not all these games leave you feeling uplifted,
and not all of them should.

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:40.690
Acceptance isn’t about feeling happy forever;
it just means an acknowledgement of a loss.

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:45.770
And for all their beauty, these worlds are
tragic; there is grief here, just as there

00:19:45.770 --> 00:19:47.440
are moments of delight.

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:49.140
These worlds have died.

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:52.400
And the world lives on.

00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:54.800
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:59.650
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:04.090
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:20:04.090 --> 00:20:25.450
See you in the next video.

